# config/fleet.env — settings that must be IDENTICAL on every node.
#
# ⚠ COMMITTED TO VERSION CONTROL. Nothing in this file is a secret, and nothing
# secret may ever be added to it. Credentials live in `.env` on the node today
# and in SSM under an instance role tomorrow; see docs/config-hardening-notes.md.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two servers' `.env` files drifted, and it was found by luck. Storage was never
# the problem — editing was. As long as the way to change a setting is "SSH in,
# edit the file, restart the containers", two nodes agree only for as long as
# nobody makes a mistake, and nothing anywhere reports when somebody does.
#
# So the fleet layer stops being server state and becomes a build artifact: one
# file, in git, reviewed like code, deployed rather than edited. Drift in these
# values is then structurally impossible — not unlikely, impossible — because
# there is exactly one copy and no editing step on the node.
#
# HOW IT IS APPLIED — DEVELOPMENT ONLY
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⚠ Only docker-compose.override.yml layers these in, so this applies to a
# CHECKOUT. The production servers carry .env and docker-compose.yml and nothing
# else; `env_file` is read by compose ON THE HOST, so naming this file there
# would stop `docker compose up` outright. Production layering is
#
#   image defaults (Settings) -> SSM -> .env
#
# and the guarantee is stronger there, not weaker: defaults ship inside the
# image, so two nodes on one tag cannot disagree. See
# docs/operator-guide/01-configuration.md.
#
# In development, docker-compose.override.yml lists three env files, and LATER
# ONES WIN:
#
#   1. config/fleet.env                 <- this file. Identical everywhere.
#   2. config/environments/<env>.env    <- per-environment, still non-secret.
#   3. .env                             <- per-node identity and secrets only.
#
# That ordering is the policy: a node CAN override a fleet value by putting it
# in its own `.env`, and doing so shows up immediately as a different
# `fleet_hash` on GET /api/config. The override is not forbidden — sometimes
# you need it at 2am — it is merely impossible to do quietly.
#
# CHANGING A VALUE HERE
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Edit, commit, deploy. Then confirm every node moved together:
#
#   scripts/check-fleet.sh https://a.example.com https://b.example.com
#
# If one node still reports the old hash, it did not get the deploy.

# ── Logging ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
LOG_LEVEL=WARNING

# ── LLM sampling ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These decide what the models actually do, which makes them the most
# consequential values in the file: a node running a different temperature
# produces different evidence and nothing else would ever say so.
LLM_PROFILES_FILE=config/llm_profiles.json
LLM_TEMPERATURE=0.1
LLM_TOP_P=0.1
LLM_MAX_TOKENS=16384
# Keep >= 10 — Gemini rejects shorter deadlines. A deadline too short for the
# work silently selects for the model that gives up soonest, which is the
# failure that looks most like success.
LLM_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=90.0

GROQ_BASE_URL=https://api.groq.com/openai/v1
DEEPSEEK_BASE_URL=https://api.deepseek.com

# ── Mail ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Hosts and mailbox names are fleet-wide; the passwords are not here.
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USE_TLS=true
IMAP_PORT=993
IMAP_MAILBOX=INBOX
IMAP_SPAM_FOLDER=Spam
# Without a timeout the socket default is *never*, and the CRM tick holds a
# fleet-wide lock while it hangs — one wedged node stops every node polling.
MAIL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30

# ── Background work ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Jobs are polled fast because somebody is watching a spinner. Concurrency
# trades against vendor rate limits: a 429 fails the candidate over to the next
# model in the chain rather than waiting, so too much of it quietly changes
# which model read the evidence. Lower this before raising it.
JOB_POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS=3
JOB_CONCURRENCY=5

LEAD_POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS=60
